| `later = true`: the request goes out on a thread of its own and `Get`
| answers `Sent { id }` at once; the `HttpResponse` arrives afterwards as a
| particle carrying `_request_id`, handled here on the program's thread when
| a loop iteration drains. Port 1 refuses at once, so the response is a
| quick `ok = false` — the shape is what this checks, not the network.
link "native_modules/http_client.so" as http

arrived = []

HttpResponse { ok, status, _request_id as id } =>
    arrived += [{ ok = ok, status = status, id = id }]
    return Seen {}

emit Get { url = "http://127.0.0.1:1/", later = true } to http get sent
assert sent ∈ Sent
assert sent.value ∈ Number
| The answer comes later, never inside the emit — but port 1 refuses so
| fast that it may already be on the ring by the next statement, so what is
| asserted is the shape and the id, not that it has not landed yet.

spins = 0
loop
    spins += 1
    emit Length { value = arrived } to core get n
    if n.value = 1
        break
    if spins > 4000000
        break

assert arrived[0].ok = false
assert arrived[0].status = 0
assert arrived[0].id = sent.value

| Without `later`, the same request still answers in place.
emit Get { url = "http://127.0.0.1:1/" } to http get r
assert r ∈ HttpResponse
assert not r.ok
